.TH LORE 1 "2026-11-23" "lore 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lore \- every command you can type, and what each one does, offline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lore
.br
.B lore
.I NAME
.br
.B lore search
.I WORDS
.br
.B lore
.B \-\-update\-pages
.br
.B lore
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lore
lists every command you can type, each name once, sorted A to Z under
section letters (names that start with a digit or a symbol go under
.BR # ).
It needs no internet; with one, each page adds what cheat.sh has for the
command (see
.BR INTERNET ).
.PP
The list holds the programs on your
.B $PATH
and in the places a
.B $PATH
often leaves out (/usr/sbin, Homebrew, MacPorts, Nix, Snap, Flatpak,
~/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, ~/go/bin, /opt/*/bin and, on macOS, the directories
in /etc/paths), the builtins and keywords of your shell (bash, zsh or fish,
from
.BR $SHELL ),
and your aliases, each shown with what it runs.
.PP
A command's page shows, top to bottom: what it is and does; VERSION, its
package, the installed and the newest known version and how to update it,
read offline from the package manager (dpkg and apt, rpm, pacman or
Homebrew's folders); HOW TO USE, its most common uses; NOTES; FILES, the
configuration, logs and data its manual names; LINKS, to its
own site and to Stack Overflow, GeeksforGeeks and Google searches; and its
manual page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I NAME
Open
.IR NAME 's
page straight away, as
.B man
.I NAME
would. Everything works from there as usual: Left or Esc go back to
the list, and Esc there quits. A name not found on this machine still
opens if lore or
.B man
has a page for it.
.TP
.I "PROGRAM SUBCOMMAND"
Open a subcommand's page, such as
.BR "lore git commit" .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-print " " \fINAME\fR
Write
.IR NAME 's
page to standard output as plain text, with the same sections, and quit;
for pipes and scripts
.RB ( "lore --print tar | less" ).
It is laid out at the terminal's width, or 80 columns in a pipe or a file,
and needs no terminal.
.TP
.BI "explain " LINE
Each word of a command line and what it means, offline:
.B lore explain """tar -xzvf a.tgz"""
gives tar's summary, then each flag
.RB ( \-x ,
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-f )
with the first sentence its man page gives it, and
.I a.tgz
as the value of
.BR \-f .
Joined short flags are split, a long option's
.I =value
is read, a pipe or a list
.RB ( | ,
.BR && ,
.BR ; )
explains every command in it, redirections say where the output goes, and
.BR sudo ,
.B env
or
.B xargs
are explained, then the command they run. Without quotes the words are
taken as they come (the shell may change some).
.TP
.BI "search " WORDS
The search's results as plain text, the best first and at most 25, one a
line: the name, the page line that matched (else the page's summary) and
its categories, separated by tabs:
.B lore search compress folder
lists tar, zip and the recipe "compress a folder". The words are searched
as in the search bar. Exits 1 when nothing matches. For scripts and
agents; needs no terminal.
.TP
.BI "\-\-completions " SHELL
Print the completion script for
.BR bash ,
.B zsh
or
.BR fish .
The packages install them already. Otherwise: in ~/.bashrc,
.BR "source <(lore --completions bash)" ;
for zsh,
.B lore --completions zsh > ~/.zfunc/_lore
with ~/.zfunc on
.BR $fpath ;
for fish,
.BR "lore --completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/lore.fish" .
Then Tab completes names
.RB ( "lore gi" ),
a program's subcommands
.RB ( "lore git " ),
recipes word by word, the options, and after
.B explain
a program's flags from its manual; zsh and fish show what each one is.
.TP
.BI "check " FILE...
Check page files, for a program that ships its own page
.RI ( NAME.md ).
Errors keep a page from being shown, and make
.B lore check
exit with 1: a title that is not
.IR NAME ,
control characters or escape sequences, invisible characters that hide or
reorder text, HTML or script, an example that runs what it downloads
.RB ( "curl ... | sh" ),
more than 64 KB, or text that is not UTF-8. Warnings: risky examples
(marked
.BR ! ),
links that are not https, have a user or a password, a punycode or a bare
address for a host, or are too long to show whole.
.TP
.B \-\-update\-pages
Download newer pages, the only time lore fetches pages: first lore's weekly
pages (every tldr page in lore's format, from lore's
.I content
release on GitHub), else tldr-pages' own zip files, converted here, in
English and in your language. Only pages and translations lore has none
of are kept, each must pass
.BR "lore check" ,
and they go in ~/.local/share/lore/updates/, replaced whole each time and
read after the built-in pages, so they never change a page lore has. It
says what it fetches on standard error, and runs even with
.IR "internet = off" ,
because you asked for it.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage text.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print lore's version.
.SH KEYS
The keys are always shown at the bottom: one line, or two on a narrow
window. On a narrow window the topics bar slides to keep the chosen topic
in sight. lore needs a window of at least 50 columns by 14 rows; a smaller
one shows its size and what is needed instead.
.SS In the list
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through the commands; the letter headers are skipped.
.TP
.B PgUp, PgDn
Move 20 commands up or down (in the search's results too).
.TP
.B w, s
Fast scroll: move up or down by the lines set in Fast scroll on the +
screen (10 at first).
.TP
.B Left, Right
Switch the topic, in the bar under the search bar: commands, recipes,
languages, docs, tools, beacon, most used, recent, bookmarks (see
.BR TOPICS ).
Round at either end.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the selected command's page.
.TP
.B /
Go to the search bar. Typing keeps the names that contain the text,
ignoring case, ranked best first instead of A to Z: the exact name, then
names starting with the text (shorter first), then names with it at the
start of a word (git rm), then anywhere. From three characters on, the
pages are searched too, by their categories, summary and examples, and
their matches follow: "compress folder" lists tar and zip, and a command
named by one of the words ("cut files") comes first among them. Words
match at the start of words ("cut" finds "cuts", not "execute"), and a
plural also finds its singular. Each result shows, after its name, the
page line that matched, cut around the match
.RB ( "[Cut out fields from stdin or files.]" ),
then its categories. With the search empty, the list is A to Z again. Common words count as their usual equivalents
(folder as directory, delete as remove). Start
with
.B @
to keep only the commands that have a lore page
.RB ( @gr ).
Esc leaves the search bar and keeps its text; Backspace erases it.
.TP
.B b
Bookmark the selected command, or take its bookmark away (see
.BR "YOUR OWN USE" ).
.TP
.B Tab, Shift+Tab
Jump to the next or the previous letter: its first command, its header on
top. Round at either end: A, B, C, ... and A again.
.TP
.B +
About lore: its version, what it found on this machine (commands, pages,
translations), where it keeps its settings and lists, its licence and
source; and the settings, changed there: Colours (Enter opens the palette
picker), Internet (on or off), History (read or not), Fast scroll (how many
lines w and s move: 5, 10, 15, 20 or 30; Enter goes to the next), Pages in
(the language pages are read in: system, which follows
.BR LANG ,
English, or any language a translation is in; the screen and the
recipes' names follow it in 12 languages, while what lore prints with -p,
search and explain stays English) and Favourite (a programming
language shown first in the languages topic), and Reset all (Enter, then
Enter again: every setting back to its default; bookmarks, recent pages,
downloaded docs and pages and the caches erased; your own pages and
palettes stay). The screen also lists the author's profiles to follow.
On a window too small for it, PgUp, PgDn, w and s scroll the screen, and
choosing a setting scrolls to it. Pages in and Favourite open a
picker: Up, Down, PgUp and PgDn choose, a letter jumps to the next choice
starting with it, Enter keeps, Esc goes back. Up and Down choose
one, Enter changes it, and it is saved at once for the next runs. +, Left or
Esc go back; p opens the palette picker too.
.TP
.B g, G
The first or the last command of the list.
.TP
.B 1 \(en 9
Go to the topic at that place in the bar.
.TP
.B B, r
Go to your bookmarks, or your recent pages; a topic with nothing in it yet
says so.
.TP
.B ?
Every key, in a box over the screen: Up, Down, PgUp and PgDn scroll it,
any other key closes it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Clear the terminal and draw the screen again (after another program
printed over it). Works everywhere.
.TP
.B Esc
Quit.
.SS On a page
.TP
.B Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn
Scroll.
.TP
.B w, s
Fast scroll: up or down by the Fast scroll lines.
.TP
.B /
Search other commands without leaving the page: the list beside it
filters as you type, and Enter or Right opens the chosen command's page in
place. Esc leaves the search for the full list.
.TP
.B Left, Esc
Back to the list, or quit when the page was opened as
.B lore
.IR NAME .
.TP
.B c
Copy one of the page's examples (on a doc's page, one of its code blocks),
only its text, never the frame around it. A language's page has nothing to
copy: its code is there to be typed by hand, which is how it is learnt.
For the others, a list opens over the page, Up and
Down choose, Enter copies (the key bar says what), Esc closes. lore asks
the terminal to copy it (the OSC 52 escape), so it works over ssh and with
no clipboard tool installed; most terminals do it, tmux with
.BR "set-clipboard on" ,
and one that does not simply ignores it.
.TP
.B b
Bookmark this page's command, or take its bookmark away; a bookmarked
page's title starts with \(**.
.TP
.B Tab, Shift+Tab
Jump to the next or the previous section (VERSION, HOW TO USE, RELATED,
\&...), round at either end.
.TP
.B g, G
The top or the bottom of the page.
.TP
.B n, N
The next or the previous line holding the search bar's text, round at
either end; the key bar says which of how many. When the words are
never side by side on the page, the lines holding any of them (of three
letters or more). Search a word with /, open a page, and n jumps to it.
.TP
.B y
Copy at once, without the list: the first example (a doc's first code
block), else the page's name. Nothing on a language's page.
.TP
.B o
The page's first link (the first under LINKS) alone in a box, whole, so
the terminal's Ctrl+click opens it easily. Any key closes the box.
.TP
.B B, r, 1 \(en 9, ?
As in the list: bookmarks, recent pages, a topic (the page closes), every
key. A search left with its text kept is dropped, so that topic's own list
shows.
.SS In the palette picker
.TP
.B Up, Down
Try the palette above or below on the whole screen.
.TP
.B Enter
Keep it, and save it for the next runs.
.TP
.B Esc
Go back to the palette from before.
.SS Anywhere
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Quit.
.SH CATEGORIES AND DANGER
In the list, each command is followed by its categories, such as
.BR "[archives files]" .
.B danger
marks a command that can destroy data or stop the system; its page warns
under the summary, and every example that can is marked with
.BR ! .
.SH LANGUAGES
Pages are shown in the language of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.B LC_MESSAGES
or
.B LANG
when lore has a translation (from tldr-pages, 41 languages); otherwise in
English.
.SH TOPICS
Each kind of thing lore knows is a topic, with a list of its own; the bar
under the search bar shows them, and Left and Right switch between them.
.B commands
is every command on this machine, A to Z;
.B recipes
the task pages (see
.BR RECIPES );
.B languages
a page per programming language (see
.BR LANGUAGES );
.B docs
documentation sets to download (see
.BR DOCS );
.B tools
a cheat sheet per tool (see
.BR TOOLS );
.B beacon
what people use most and what is rising (see
.BR BEACON );
.BR "most used" ,
.B recent
and
.B bookmarks
your own (see
.BR "YOUR OWN USE" ).
A topic with nothing in it yet is not shown. A search looks in every topic.
.SH LANGUAGES
The
.B languages
topic holds the pages of Learn X in Y minutes
.RI ( https://learnxinyminutes.com ):
for each of about 200 programming languages, tools and formats, a short
introduction and one commented file that walks through how it works, to be
read from top to bottom. A page opens like a file in
.BR bat :
the introduction as text, then the file in a frame, its name on top, each
line numbered, comments, strings, numbers and keywords in colour, and a line
too long for the panel wrapped under its number. Tab and Shift+Tab jump
between the file's parts (its banner comments). In your language when the
site has a translation. A language named like a command on this machine
gets
.I (language)
after its name. The pages are unchanged, by their contributors, under
CC BY-SA 3.0.
.SH DOCS
The
.B docs
topic lists every documentation set of DevDocs
.RI ( https://devdocs.io ),
about 850 for languages, frameworks and tools: the 50 most asked about on
Stack Overflow first, then all of them A to Z. Press
.B d
on a set to download it in the background, to read offline; it is kept in
~/.local/share/lore/docs/.
.B x
deletes it. Enter on a downloaded set lists its pages by type, and
.B /
searches them (with no set open,
.B /
searches the sets); Enter opens one at its own part, its HTML shown as
text. Esc goes back to the sets. The sets are by their projects, under
their licences, credited on each page.
.SH BEACON
The
.B beacon
topic lists the commands trending this month, the most used ones, then
the most used per category
.RI ( "most used for archives" ),
with Tab and Shift+Tab between these headings. After each name: its rank,
its rise (\(ua 2.4\(mu this month) and
.I not installed
when it is not on this machine; such a command is listed when lore has a
page for it, to read before installing it.
.PP
The numbers are public, and nothing about your own use is sent: the
machines that use a package regularly, from Debian's popularity contest
(the base system, on nearly every machine, left out), and Homebrew's
installs asked for by name over a year and over the last 30 days (their
ratio is the rise). A table of them is built into lore, so the topic works
offline. With Internet on, a newer one is fetched at most once a week,
for the next start: lore's own copy first, else from the two sources, and
kept in ~/.cache/lore/beacon.tsv.
.SH RECIPES
The
.B recipes
topic holds 100 short task pages that chain commands: free disk space, what is
using a port, why won't a service start, add a user, find files containing
a text, copy files to and from a server, read a file line by line, and
more, most of them the terminal tasks people ask about most. Each shows numbered STEPS (risky ones
marked), the commands it USES, and notes for other distributions and
macOS. The search finds them by name and by what they say;
.B c
copies a step's command. They are in lore's repository, recipes/, one
Markdown file each.
.SH TOOLS
The
.B tools
topic holds a cheat sheet per tool: the everyday tasks people look up for
networking, language environments, git, databases, containers, tmux and
vim, servers, keys and certificates, the cloud command lines and files.
Each shows its TASKS in groups (Tab jumps from group to group; risky ones
marked), its notes, related commands and links. The search finds them by
name and by what they say;
.B c
copies a task's command. A tool named like a command here is listed as
.IR "NAME " (tool).
They are in lore's repository, tools/, one Markdown file each.
.SH YOUR OWN USE
Three topics are yours:
.B bookmarks
(added and removed with
.BR b ),
.B recent
(the last 10 pages opened) and
.B most used
(the 50 commands your shell history ran most, most first). In a search, among names that match the same way,
the ones you run most come first. A dim dot after a name marks a command
your history never ran.
.PP
The history is read from ~/.bash_history, ~/.zsh_history, ~/.histfile,
~/.local/share/fish/fish_history and
.BR $HISTFILE ,
whichever exist. lore only reads it, keeps nothing from it, and sends
nothing anywhere;
.I "history = off"
in the settings file (or History on the + screen) stops it reading it.
.SH COLOURS
On the + screen, Enter on Colours (or
.BR p )
opens the palette picker; Enter saves it in the settings file (see
.BR FILES ).
.B LORE_PALETTE
names one for a single run, over the saved one.
.PP
Built in:
.B terminal
(the default, the terminal's own colours), Catppuccin, Dracula, Nord,
Gruvbox, Solarized, Tokyo Night, One, Ros\('e Pine, Monokai, GitHub,
Everforest, Kanagawa and Ayu, most in a dark and a light variant; and, for
colour blindness and low vision, Modus (vivendi and operandi, every colour at
least 7:1, with deuteranopia and tritanopia versions), GitHub colour-blind
and high-contrast, and Okabe-Ito. Every palette but
.B terminal
paints its own background. An unknown name gives the default; case does
not matter.
.PP
Your own: a file
.I NAME.palette
in
.I ~/.config/lore/palettes/
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lore/palettes/ ),
read at start; one with a built-in palette's name takes its place, and one
that does not read is skipped. One
.I "key = value"
a line:
.BR background ,
.BR text ,
.BR muted ,
.BR selection ,
.BR primary ,
.BR secondary ,
.BR accent ,
.BR danger ,
.B code
and
.BR warning ,
each
.I #rrggbb
or a terminal colour name
.RB ( cyan ,
.BR reset ...).
The palettes/ folder of lore's repository has the built-in ones and the
format in full.
.SH INTERNET
A dot on the search bar,
.BR "\(bu internet" ,
is green when cht.sh can be reached and red when not; the about screen
.RB ( + )
says the same. A command's page then has an INTERNET section before
MANUAL, with the dot, and that command's community cheat sheet from
.IR https://cht.sh/NAME ,
fetched in the background when the page opens and kept for a day in
~/.cache/lore/internet/.
.PP
It is raw text, written by anyone and not checked: it can be changed at
any time. lore takes out every control and escape character, shows it as
it came behind a bar, never turns it into links, never copies it
.RB ( c
copies only the page's own examples) and never runs it. Read it, and copy
from it with care.
.PP
.I "internet = off"
in the settings file, or Internet turned off on the + screen: lore makes
no request on its own (no cheat sheet, no beacon table), and the dot is
red. Only
.B lore \-\-update\-pages
still downloads, when you type it.
.SH LINKS
Every URL is shown whole, on a line of its own, so the terminal can open
it: Ctrl+click in Konsole and most terminals. lore never opens a link
itself, shows only https links, and never asks you to log in anywhere or
for any account. Check a link before you open it: anyone can add pages.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lore/config
.TQ
.I ~/.config/lore/config
lore's settings, written when a setting is changed in the app (the +
screen, or p for colours): one
.I "key = value"
a line,
.B #
starts a comment:
.IR "palette = NAME" ,
.I "history = off"
and
.IR "internet = off" .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/lore/internet/NAME.txt
cht.sh's answer for
.IR NAME ,
kept for a day
.RB ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME ).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/lore/beacon.tsv
The newest beacon table fetched, used at the next start
.RB ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME ).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/lore/bookmarks
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/lore/bookmarks
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/lore/recent
Your bookmarks and recently opened pages, one name a line.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lore/content/NAME.md
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/lore/updates/NAME.md
.TQ
.I /usr/local/share/lore/content/NAME.md
.TQ
.I /usr/share/lore/content/NAME.md
A page for
.IR NAME ,
and its translations in
.IR i18n/LANG/NAME.md
under the same folders. Yours, in ~/.local/share/lore/content, come first
and may replace a page built into lore. The updates folder is what
.B lore \-\-update\-pages
fetched: used only for a command, or a translation, lore has none of. The
other two are where programs
install a page for their own command: lore uses them only for a command it
has no page for, so no package can rewrite another command's page. Every
one must pass
.B lore check
or it is not shown. None of these folders needs to exist. The format is in
content/README.md in lore's repository.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal quit; 1 when there is no terminal to draw on (a pipe, or
.B ssh
without
.BR \-t ),
on an unknown option, or when
.I NAME
has no command, lore page or manual page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR whatis (1),
.BR apropos (1)
.PP
https://github.com/guhhammer/lore
